// The floor the shipped userscript is held to, and the only half of
// `docs/browser-support.md` a compiler can hold by itself.
//
// `tsconfig.json` is `lib: ["ESNext", "DOM"]`, which is right for `tools/` and
// `tests/` — they run in Bun, on this machine, at whatever version is
// installed. It is wrong for `src/` and `libs/`, which run in somebody else's
// browser: `build.ts` bundles with `minify: false` and no `target: "esNN"`, so
// nothing is downlevelled and nothing is polyfilled. The ES level of the source
// is the ES level a player's browser has to have.
//
// So the narrowing is the guard, and it is two narrowings rather than one.
// Reach for `toSorted`, `Object.hasOwn` or `structuredClone` in shipped code and
// the `lib` refuses it by name; write syntax a floor browser cannot parse and
// the `target` refuses that. Both are §9.3's compiler-instead-of-a-linter,
// applied to the two axes a compiler can see. The DOM and CSS halves it cannot
// see are held by `tests/tools/browser-support.test.ts` instead — and the
// pattern constructs the `target` check misses are held by nobody yet, which
// the second comment below states rather than leaves to be discovered.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ES2022 rather than ES2021, and exactly one thing decides it: `ErrorOptions`
    // in `src/core/margometer-error.ts`. Under ES2021 the two errors are
    // `Cannot find name 'ErrorOptions'` and `Expected 0-1 arguments, but got 2`.
    // No shipped caller passes a `cause` — it is the declaration that needs the
    // lib, not the traffic — but a floor is about what has to be there, not
    // about what is currently used.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],

    // The same floor on the other axis, holding a different thing: `lib` decides
    // which library members exist, `target` decides which **syntax** is allowed —
    // and a regular expression's syntax is checked against this and against
    // nothing else. Inherited from `tsconfig.json` it was `ESNext`, so
    // `/[\p{ASCII}--[a-z]]/v` in `src/` typechecked clean; with this line it is
    // `error TS1501: This regular expression flag is only available when
    // targeting 'es2024' or later`. Measured 2026-08-27 by putting that pattern
    // into `src/game/game-dictionary.ts` and restoring the file from a copy
    // (`docs/specs/2026-08-27-a-pattern-the-floor-never-covered.md`).
    //
    // Nothing is emitted here — `noEmit` is inherited — so this narrows what the
    // compiler accepts and downlevels nothing. `build.ts` still bundles with no
    // target, which is what keeps the source's ES level the player's.
    //
    // ⚠️ **Partial, and it misses the cheap mistakes.** Dropped to `ES2017` for
    // the measurement, the compiler refuses `/(?<name>x)/` by name and accepts
    // both `/(?<=x)y/` and `/\p{L}/u` without a word. So of the pattern
    // constructs above this floor it catches the `v` flag and misses lookbehind
    // (Safari 16.4) and the `(?i:…)` modifier (Safari 26) — versions from MDN
    // `browser-compat-data`, read 2026-08-27. Those two are held by nothing, and
    // a pattern above the floor does not degrade the way §9.9 promises of CSS
    // and the DOM: it is an early SyntaxError, so the bundle never parses and the
    // reader sees no panel at all.
    "target": "ES2022",

    // ⚠️ **Load-bearing, and measured rather than assumed.** With this line
    // removed, `lib: ["ES2021", "DOM"]` typechecks clean: `@types/bun` is
    // ambient, it carries its own lib references, and it puts back everything
    // this file exists to take away. The narrowing would then pass while proving
    // nothing — the exact shape §6.1 already records, where type information
    // `bun.lock` does not name makes the local gate disagree with CI.
    //
    // Nothing under `src/` or `libs/` needs Bun's globals: `performance.now()`
    // in `libs/elapsed-spans.ts` is `DOM`. If something ever does, narrow
    // `include` — do not put `types` back.
    "types": []
  },

  // `libs/` whole, not just the files that ship today. Anything there is
  // importable by `src/` tomorrow — that is what the bottom layer is for — so
  // holding the browser floor over all of it is the answer that does not depend
  // on who happens to import what this week.
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "libs/**/*.ts"]
}
